Optional embedded terminals
===========================

The existing command entry, output tabs, command history, macros, aliases and
background-task output remain the default interface. Embedded terminals are
additional interactive PTY shells; terminal input is interpreted by the shell,
not by emelFM2. Installing VTE does not enable this feature.

Build one of the following configurations:

  make WITH_GTK2=1 WITH_GTK3=0 WITH_VTE=0
  make WITH_GTK2=1 WITH_GTK3=0 WITH_VTE=1
  make WITH_GTK2=0 WITH_GTK3=1 WITH_VTE=0
  make WITH_GTK2=0 WITH_GTK3=1 WITH_VTE=1

WITH_VTE defaults to 0. GTK 2 terminals require pkg-config module vte >= 0.28
(the tested legacy API is 0.28.2, Debian/Ubuntu package libvte-dev). GTK 3
terminals require vte-2.91 >= 0.48 (libvte-2.91-dev). Missing dependencies and
conflicting GTK selectors are errors; a requested terminal never silently
falls back to the console. Only the selected adapter is compiled.

Use separate clean checkouts for simultaneous configurations: build.h and the
binary are shared within a checkout. Sequential feature changes invalidate the
build header, objects, dependency files and plugins. Use make clean when changing
installation paths or compiler infrastructure. Use the SAME feature flags for
make and make install. Without VTE there are no VTE package checks, headers or
linked libraries. NEW_COMMAND=0 and NEW_COMMAND=1 are both supported.

Using terminals
---------------

The lower area has two side-by-side outputs, one for each directory pane. Each
starts on Command log. Use the New terminal icon to start a new session in
that pane's directory. There is no automatic startup
session and no automatic directory synchronization. Remote/unmounted locations
cannot be used; select an existing local directory first. Each terminal keeps its
own startup directory, scrollback and shell history. Navigating the file panes
never writes a cd command into a terminal.

Each pane has one flat tab strip containing Command log, its terminals, a New
terminal icon and a View actions menu. Copy, Paste, Insert selected paths and
Restart are available in the terminal menu; exited sessions also have inline
Restart and Close controls. Each terminal tab has
its own close button. Tab titles show user@folder, with the current folder's
basename. Starting, running, failed and exited states appear in the title's
tooltip; startup errors and exit messages appear below the terminal. Running
terminals have no startup-path bar. Failed/exited tabs stay available for
inspection; Restart uses the last verified local directory, or the original
startup directory when the current location cannot be verified. Its tooltip
explains the choice. The title tooltip includes the full current path and any
reported remote host, without a permanent path bar.
Application logs go to the initiating pane's output view, including while a
terminal has focus. The Edit action is available only for application logs;
terminal context menus offer terminal-specific actions. VTE builds have one
permanent Command log per pane and do not create or detach additional log tabs.
When older multi-log layouts or per-main-tab outputs are combined, their text
and formatting are merged into the retained log with separators; running
commands continue there. Builds without VTE keep the native output-tab controls.

In Configuration / commands, enable "keep output and terminals with file-pane
tabs" (terminal-per-tab) to give each main tab its own pair of outputs and
terminals. This requires file-pane tabs and defaults to off. Switching tabs keeps
shells and background output running. Closing a main tab asks before hanging up
its live terminals; native command output continues in the remaining tab.
Disabling this option or file-pane tabs combines the existing outputs and
terminals into the shared area, preserving their contents and running sessions.

Configurable actions (usable in keybindings, toolbars and menus):
  terminal.open_here
  terminal.focus
  terminal.close
  terminal.restart
  terminal.insert_paths
  terminal.show_folder
  terminal.hide_tools
  terminal.expand_tools
  terminal.next_tab
  terminal.previous_tab

For side-by-side file panes, the tools divider follows the file divider; dragging
it also adjusts the files. Stacked file panes keep a separate side-by-side tools
split. A themed focus border identifies the tools pane receiving keyboard input.
Hide returns to the file list without changing its cursor or selection. Show
restores the selected tabs and height. Expand fills the area with the selected
view; the same action restores both tools areas and the file lists.

Show terminal folder in pane navigates only on request and only to an observed,
existing local working directory. Remote paths never become local navigation
commands, even if the path exists locally. Cycling actions wrap in both directions
and may be assigned shortcuts in the file-manager context; they do not reserve
keys used by terminal programs.

Focused terminals receive Ctrl+C, Ctrl+Z, Tab and function keys directly.
Ctrl+Shift+C copies, Ctrl+Shift+V pastes, Ctrl+Shift+F6 returns focus to the
active file pane. Use a custom binding for terminal.focus
to return. The emelFM2 command entry retains its own completion and history.

Preferences under commands/terminal apply to new and restarted sessions:
  terminal-shell       executable path, without arguments; empty uses $SHELL,
                       falling back to /bin/sh. Shells are started with -i.
  terminal-scrollback  retained lines (default 10000)
  terminal-font        default Monospace 10
  terminal-foreground  default #dddddd
  terminal-background  default #202020

Use an executable path to sh, bash, dash, zsh, ksh, mksh or fish for pathname
insertion. Other interactive executables may be started, but generated path
insertion is disabled because their quoting rules are unknown. Selected paths
are absolute (so leading hyphens do not become options) and shell-quoted, with
no final Enter. Insert them at a shell prompt where arguments are expected.
No emelFM2 macros, wildcard expansion or aliases are applied. Clipboard paste
retains the backend's normal terminal paste behavior. The legacy backend and
VTE versions before 0.68 lack the paste_text API; generated paths use direct
PTY input.
Legacy VTE reports that a shell exited but cannot supply its exit status.
Titles refresh twice a second, including in hidden main tabs. On Linux, the
foreground process's effective UID and /proc cwd track local user/directory
changes, nested shells and directory renames. PTY wrappers such as sudo are
followed to their inner foreground process where accessible. Recognized user@host:/path (or
:~/path) shell titles also provide user/location information; GTK 3 additionally
reads OSC 7 directory URIs. No shell startup files or prompt commands are changed.
Reports from the previous foreground process are discarded when context changes.
If another user's cwd cannot be read, its shell must report the path; otherwise
the folder is shown as ? rather than the previous user's directory. Remote
sessions and systems without /proc need shell reports for live information.
A remote title reporting only ~ identifies home, not its actual directory name;
an absolute shell title or OSC 7 URI supplies the folder name. Arbitrary application
titles are ignored, and reported metadata is display-only, not proof of identity.
Hyperlink actions are not implemented.

Closing a running/starting terminal or quitting with live sessions asks before
hanging up the shell and closing its PTY. A forced application shutdown closes
terminals without a dialog. VTE owns terminal-child reaping even if a tab is
closed during asynchronous startup. Jobs that deliberately detach from their
terminal can survive a hangup; this is not a process-tree kill operation.

Native command children have separate per-PID monitors, retaining stop/continue
notifications. They do not install SIGCHLD handlers or reap arbitrary children.
A monitor thread is used because NEW_COMMAND's synchronous execution can block
the GTK main context. GTK and VTE operations stay on the main thread.

Packaging and validation
------------------------

Default Debian packages remain GTK 2 without VTE. Optional build profiles:

  DEB_BUILD_PROFILES=pkg.emelfm2.vte dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b
  DEB_BUILD_PROFILES='pkg.emelfm2.gtk3 pkg.emelfm2.vte' dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b

Use pkg.emelfm2.gtk3 alone for GTK 3 without terminals. Install build dependencies
with the same profiles (e.g. apt-get build-dep -Ppkg.emelfm2.vte .). Profile
restrictions choose exactly the matching GTK and VTE development package;
dh_shlibdeps derives runtime dependencies from the actual linked binaries.

AppImage dependency selection: tools/appimage-dependencies.sh GTK WITH_VTE.
Stage a feature-specific build in a clean checkout using:
  tools/stage-appimage.sh /tmp/emelfm2-AppDir 3 1
Then run linuxdeploy with DEPLOY_GTK_VERSION=3 and its GTK plugin against that
AppDir. Use 2 for legacy builds. linuxdeploy follows the binary's VTE dependency;
never reuse AppDirs or plugin objects from the other GTK generation. Default
release jobs explicitly use GTK 2 / WITH_VTE=0. Optional validation artifacts
are distinct from releases and are never automatically published.

The Validate build configurations workflow always builds GTK 2 and GTK 3 with
VTE disabled and both NEW_COMMAND values. Its manual include_vte input defaults
to false; enabling it adds both terminal backends. Ubuntu 22.04 provides both
legacy VTE 0.28.2 and modern VTE. Dependency installation failures fail the job.

Local tests after building the chosen configuration:
  GTK_PACKAGE=gtk+-2.0 tests/run-terminal.sh
  GTK_PACKAGE=gtk+-3.0 tests/run-terminal.sh
  GTK_PACKAGE=gtk+-2.0 tests/run-vte-ui.sh
  GTK_PACKAGE=gtk+-3.0 tests/run-vte-ui.sh
  GTK_PACKAGE=gtk+-2.0 tests/run-terminal-title.sh
  GTK_PACKAGE=gtk+-3.0 tests/run-terminal-title.sh
  GTK_PACKAGE=gtk+-3.0 sh tests/run-command-wildcards.sh

Terminal tests require Xvfb, xauth, dbus-run-session and a font. They exercise
real PTYs, concurrent native/terminal exits, foreign-child ownership,
stop/continue, spawn failure/cancellation, session close/restart, startup cwd,
interactive insertion, Unicode, special-character quoting, top/htop and resize. Available sh,
bash, dash, zsh and fish executables are tested separately.
The VTE UI test checks toolbar icons/tooltips, tab close buttons, context menus,
per-pane output routing, per-main-tab sessions, option toggling and close prompts.
Title tests require Linux /proc, bash and util-linux script. They cover directory
changes/renames, nested shells and PTYs, reported usernames, OSC 7 on GTK 3,
Unicode and control characters, and stale metadata when returning to a shell.

Manual acceptance checklist for both backends:
- Run less/vim, resize, Ctrl+C, Ctrl+Z and fg; check job control and key routing.
- Produce long Unicode output; scroll, select, copy and paste; open several tabs.
- Run file operations and background commands while a terminal application runs.
- Verify native log creation/detach controls are absent in VTE builds.
- Change preferences and pane layout; confirm live sessions remain usable.
- Cancel and accept close/quit with active jobs and during startup.
- Test X11 for both GTK generations, and GTK 3 Wayland where supported by emelFM2.
- Smoke-test installed Debian and AppImage artifacts; inspect linked libraries
  for both the binary and bundled plugins, including a no-VTE dependency image.

API references:
https://gnome.pages.gitlab.gnome.org/vte/gtk3/method.Terminal.spawn_async.html
https://gnome.pages.gitlab.gnome.org/vte/gtk3/method.Terminal.spawn_with_fds_async.html
Legacy API/signals were checked against the libvte-dev 0.28.2 headers.
The modern adapter was also compiled against the official VTE 0.48.4 headers.

Initial validation (2026-09-19)
-------------------------------

All eight GTK 2/3 x WITH_VTE=0/1 x NEW_COMMAND=0/1 configurations built in
separate source copies and passed wildcard and full-application smoke tests.
VTE builds passed the PTY, ownership and session tests. Fullscreen top/htop,
resize and shell job control were tested on X11 with both backends. Libraries:
GTK 2.24.33 / VTE 0.28.2 and GTK 3.24.33 / VTE 0.68.0. The GTK 3 adapter also
passed compilation against the official 0.48.4 headers (not a 0.48 runtime).

Default GTK 2 and optional GTK 3/VTE Debian packages built, and extracted
executables passed smoke tests with their matching packaged plugins. Default
Debian dependencies contain no VTE; optional dependencies contain only modern
VTE and GTK 3. The GTK 3/VTE AppImage staging script was exercised. Final
AppImage execution and native Wayland remain manual acceptance checks.

A simulated missing-VTE pkg-config wrapper verified that disabled builds make
no VTE probes and enabled builds fail clearly. The CI jobs also select only the
development packages needed by their explicit configuration.
